@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1224 = private unnamed_addr constant [945 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for calls to `re.sub`, `re.subn`, and `re.split` that pass `count`,\0A`maxsplit`, or `flags` as positional arguments.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APassing `count`, `maxsplit`, or `flags` as positional arguments to\0A`re.sub`, `re.subn`, or `re.split` can lead to confusion, as most methods in\0Athe `re` module accept `flags` as the third positional argument, while\0A`re.sub`, `re.subn`, and `re.split` have different signatures.\0A\0AInstead, pass `count`, `maxsplit`, and `flags` as keyword arguments.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport re\0A\0Are.split(\22pattern\22, \22replacement\22, 1)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport re\0A\0Are.split(\22pattern\22, \22replacement\22, maxsplit=1)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `re.sub`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.sub)\0A- [Python documentation: `re.subn`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.subn)\0A- [Python documentation: `re.split`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.split)\0A", align 1
