@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1231 = private unnamed_addr constant [1015 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for function calls that use star-argument unpacking after providing a\0Akeyword argument\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, you can use star-argument unpacking to pass a list or tuple of\0Aarguments to a function.\0A\0AProviding a star-argument after a keyword argument can lead to confusing\0Abehavior, and is only supported for backwards compatibility.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef foo(x, y, z):\0A    return x, y, z\0A\0A\0Afoo(1, 2, 3)  # (1, 2, 3)\0Afoo(1, *[2, 3])  # (1, 2, 3)\0A# foo(x=1, *[2, 3])  # TypeError\0A# foo(y=2, *[1, 3])  # TypeError\0Afoo(z=3, *[1, 2])  # (1, 2, 3)  # No error, but confusing!\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef foo(x, y, z):\0A    return x, y, z\0A\0A\0Afoo(1, 2, 3)  # (1, 2, 3)\0Afoo(x=1, y=2, z=3)  # (1, 2, 3)\0Afoo(*[1, 2, 3])  # (1, 2, 3)\0Afoo(*[1, 2], 3)  # (1, 2, 3)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Calls](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#calls)\0A- [Disallow iterable argument unpacking after a keyword argument?](https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/82741)\0A", align 1
