@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1255 = private unnamed_addr constant [689 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for a shebang directive that is not at the beginning of the file.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, a shebang (also known as a hashbang) is the first line of a\0Ascript, which specifies the interpreter that should be used to run the\0Ascript.\0A\0AThe shebang's `#!` prefix must be the first two characters of a file. If\0Athe shebang is not at the beginning of the file, it will be ignored, which\0Ais likely a mistake.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afoo = 1\0A#!/usr/bin/env python3\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A#!/usr/bin/env python3\0Afoo = 1\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Executable Python Scripts](https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/appendix.html#executable-python-scripts)\0A", align 1
