@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1377 = private unnamed_addr constant [1393 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for undocumented magic method definitions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AMagic methods (methods with names that start and end with double\0Aunderscores) are used to implement operator overloading and other special\0Abehavior. Such methods should be documented via docstrings to\0Aoutline their behavior.\0A\0AGenerally, magic method docstrings should describe the method's behavior,\0Aarguments, side effects, exceptions, return values, and any other\0Ainformation that may be relevant to the user.\0A\0AIf the codebase adheres to a standard format for method docstrings, follow\0Athat format for consistency.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Cat(Animal):\0A    def __str__(self) -> str:\0A        return f\22Cat: {self.name}\22\0A\0A\0Acat = Cat(\22Dusty\22)\0Aprint(cat)  # \22Cat: Dusty\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Cat(Animal):\0A    def __str__(self) -> str:\0A        \22\22\22Return a string representation of the cat.\22\22\22\0A        return f\22Cat: {self.name}\22\0A\0A\0Acat = Cat(\22Dusty\22)\0Aprint(cat)  # \22Cat: Dusty\22\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.pydocstyle.ignore-decorators`\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 257 \E2\80\93 Docstring Conventions](https://peps.python.org/pep-0257/)\0A- [PEP 287 \E2\80\93 reStructuredText Docstring Format](https://peps.python.org/pep-0287/)\0A- [NumPy Style Guide](https://numpydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/format.html)\0A- [Google Style Python Docstrings](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#s3.8-comments-and-docstrings)\0A", align 1
