@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1381 = private unnamed_addr constant [1043 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for undocumented public class definitions, for nested classes.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APublic classes should be documented via docstrings to outline their\0Apurpose and behavior.\0A\0ANested classes do not inherit the docstring of their enclosing class, so\0Athey should have their own docstrings.\0A\0AIf the codebase adheres to a standard format for class docstrings, follow\0Athat format for consistency.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    \22\22\22Class Foo.\22\22\22\0A\0A    class Bar: ...\0A\0A\0Abar = Foo.Bar()\0Abar.__doc__  # None\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    \22\22\22Class Foo.\22\22\22\0A\0A    class Bar:\0A        \22\22\22Class Bar.\22\22\22\0A\0A\0Abar = Foo.Bar()\0Abar.__doc__  # \22Class Bar.\22\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 257 \E2\80\93 Docstring Conventions](https://peps.python.org/pep-0257/)\0A- [PEP 287 \E2\80\93 reStructuredText Docstring Format](https://peps.python.org/pep-0287/)\0A- [NumPy Style Guide](https://numpydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/format.html)\0A- [Google Style Python Docstrings](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#s3.8-comments-and-docstrings)\0A", align 1
